  "details": "While this is largely starting as a complaint about the VRCStation editor, this is going to extend to any and all custom editors that VRChat has been using for their built-in components.\n\nThe editor for the VRCStation script, or VRCPlayerStationEditor3.cs, long and short, shouldn't exist. This isn't to say that VRChat shouldn't use custom editors, they're wonderful and can be very powerful and necessary in specific circumstances. My complaint is however with it's implementation. The editor gives access to every single serializable public variable that the VRCStation class has line for line, making no adjustments to the naming, order, or accessibility of any of these variables. Simply being a proxy for these variables isn't a problem, the problem is that having this generic editor comes with a host of problems that the creator did not resolve.\n\nFirst and foremost, there is absolutely no variable saving features, meaning all the values are exposed, but never applied back onto the script. If the last thing the player did was make some sort of adjustments to the chair in question before hitting play or building a test client, none of those changes actually get stored. You would need to \"dirty\" the scene in some other way for these changes to properly store.\n\nSecondly, having a custom editor breaks support for Unity's built in multi-edit feature. This is of course easily alleviated with with the class attribute [CanEditMultipleObjects] placed alongside the line for CustomEditor, but this script does not have that, meaning if you want to mass-change the settings on an audience of seats, you have to make this change to each individually. If the editor was being used to obfuscate a direct link to the dll file for the VRCStation script, that would be, although unnecessary, somewhat understandable. The problem is that this is not the case across the board. Most of the VRChat components do not have custom editors, such as Avatar Pedestals, Video Players, Midi Handlers, and Object Pools, while Pickups, Spatial Audio, Midi Listeners, and Stations all lose out on multi-edit due to this line not existing in their editor.\n\nFocusing on the VRC Spatial Audio Source, this one thankfully has proper serialization and stores the values correctly when you've edited them. The problems come with it also not having MultiEdit enabled. Audio Sources are something that you'll want to have multiple of in a lot of situations, and making changes to the settings of all of them can be a bit annoying. This is then compounded by the minor annoyance of the \"Advanced Options\" dropdown not being saved between different GameObjects, and should probably be stored as an EditorPref to make it's visibility consistent. This dropdown also has the minor annoyance of not allowing for opening the dropdown by clicking the words itself, though this is at this point just nitpicking. Foldouts can take a third variable telling it to open when clicked or not, which would be a simple change from \"EditorGUILayout.Foldout(showAdvancedOptions, \"Advanced Options\");\" to \"EditorGUILayout.Foldout(showAdvancedOptions, \"Advanced Options\", true);\"\n\nWith that, I think I've complained enough, so I will let you go about your day. Thanks!",
